index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..a87c6e01d50b90bca7daa5af792239d3a7b738a6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,208 @@
+# arXiv 1401.1331 — noisy short-interval polynomial interpolation
+
+**Status:** locally verified, but **not yet shippable**. The mandatory OpenRouter
+hardening and G9 runs were attempted and received only account-level HTTP 403
+`Key limit exceeded` errors. Those calls are preserved in the three transcript
+files; they are not counted as model failures, and G9 correctly remains false.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | B — no-tool compression |
+| Native domain | algebra |
+| Object regime | finite field |
+| Computational core | linear algebra |
+| Certificate form | polynomial |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+| Intended intuition | change of variables: recognize the marked arithmetic progression and use forward differences in a scaled falling-factorial basis |
+
+## What the family asks
+
+The source is Garcia-Morchon, Rietman, Shparlinski, and Tolhuizen,
+[“Interpolation and Approximation of Polynomials in Finite Fields over a Short
+Interval from Noisy Values”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.1331). An instance gives a
+prime `p`, a short interval, noisy residues `s` satisfying circular distance
+`|s-f(t)|_p <= Delta`, and several admissible progression steps. The solver must
+return the polynomial over `F_p` in one scaled falling-factorial basis
+`sum b_j binom(X/r,j)`. Verification evaluates this polynomial at every displayed
+point with exact modular arithmetic and checks every noise inequality. It never
+reads the planted answer.
+
+Generation is inverse. A step and a full-degree Newton coefficient vector are
+sampled first (equivalently, values on one progression are sampled and the exact
+forward-difference identity is inverted), then all noisy observations are formed.
+Every candidate progression has the same shape and every individual error uses
+the same `0..Delta` distribution. Only the useful progression has a common-error
+correlation; its residues consequently share the displayed marker class.
+
+## Why Track B, not Track A
+
+Section 1.2 defines circular residue distance and explains why coefficients below
+degree `k` must be known; when `h^k <= Delta`, recovery is not identifiable. This
+family uses `k=1`, publishes `f(0)=0`, and stays away from that regime. Lemma 7 and
+Theorem 9 are the decisive Step-0 facts: for fixed dimension the paper uses exact
+closest-vector decoding and obtains deterministic polynomial-time recovery from
+random short-interval samples. A Track A claim would therefore be false.
+
+The retained reference implementation performs exact signed-error enumeration
+and Newton interpolation, a fixed-dimensional bounded-distance decoder with cost
+`O((2*Delta+1)^d (d^2 + N d))`. On eight shipping instances it tried 762,819
+candidates, used 44,291,118 counted exact operations (5,536,390 mean), solved 8/8,
+and took 4.05 seconds total (0.51 seconds mean). The compact route uses Section
+5.2's identity `d(x)=sum D^i d(0) binom(x,i)`: scan the 32 equal-shaped
+progressions modulo the marker, then build one six-level difference table. That
+is 245 counted operations. Millions of exact operations are easy for a program
+but unavailable in the evaluated no-tool context; 245 simple reductions and
+subtractions fit the stated cap if the structural clue is found.
+
+The paper also identifies regimes deliberately not claimed here: whole-field and
+long-interval variants admit earlier exponential-sum-based methods, Theorem 9
+assumes random points and fixed degree, and Section 6 gives practical lattice
+approximation heuristics. This generator is a structured native subfamily, not a
+sample from Theorem 9's random-point distribution.
+
+## Worked demo (`seed=2`)
+
+This is the complete rendered demo instance:
+
+```text
+Noisy polynomial interpolation over a short interval
+
+Let p = 1000003, a prime.  The finite field F_p is represented by the
+integers 0 through p-1.  For residues a,b define their circular distance as
+min((a-b) mod p, (b-a) mod p), an integer from 0 through (p-1)/2.
+
+An unknown polynomial f over F_p has exact degree d = 2 and
+known constant value f(0)=0.  At every displayed integer t, the displayed
+residue s satisfies circular_distance(f(t),s) <= Delta, where Delta =
+1.  All t lie in the closed interval
+[-94, 94].  Points do not repeat.
+
+Return f in one of the following scaled falling-factorial bases.  Choose one
+signed, nonzero step r from this admissible list (order has no significance):
+-47, -37, 19
+
+Your coefficients must be exactly d+1 canonical residues b_0,...,b_d with
+0 <= b_j < p, b_0=0, and b_d != 0.  They denote
+
+    f(X) = sum from j=0 to d of b_j * binom(X/r, j)  in F_p,
+
+where X/r means X times the inverse of r modulo p, binom(Y,0)=1, and
+binom(Y,j)=Y(Y-1)...(Y-j+1)/j! in F_p.  Different admissible steps may give
+different coefficient lists for the same polynomial; any one that meets every
+observation is valid.  The auxiliary integer marker is M = 10.
+
+Observations are lines "t s" in arbitrary order:
+-94 421332
+0 1
+-74 943867
+19 947671
+-37 17986
+38 667251
+-47 950062
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as one JSON object with
+exactly the keys "step" and "coefficients".  Integers are decimal and the
+coefficient list is ordered b_0 through b_d.
+Example: <answer>{"step": 17, "coefficients": [0, 4, 9]}</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The three residues at `0,19,38` end in the same digit. Their first and second
+forward differences modulo `1000003` give:
+
+```text
+<answer>{"step":19,"coefficients":[0,947670,771916]}</answer>
+verify(...) -> (True, "ok")
+```
+
+Changing `947670` to `947671` gives
+`(False, "sample t=-94 has circular error 210521, exceeding Delta 1 ...")`.
+A person can solve this demo on paper with two difference rows and seven checks.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | candidate progressions `n` | degree | Delta | prime bits | observations | intended ops | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 3 | 2 | 1 | 20 | 7 | 12 | hand example |
+| easy | 32 | 6 | 3 | 61 | 193 | 245 | provisional shipping preset; oracle blocked |
+| medium | 35 | 6 | 4 | 61 | 211 | 266 | not reached |
+| hard | 38 | 6 | 4 | 127 | 229 | 287 | not reached |
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result | measured evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 preset-seed instances verify; 12/12 JSON round trips |
+| G2 | pass | drop, swap, duplicate, empty, and bad-step corruptions all rejected with distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged JSON recovered from prose and a Markdown fence |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware random candidates valid; declared space about `4.81e111` |
+| G5 | pass | shipping density 0/200,000; reference 44,291,118 ops, 4.05 s total |
+| G6 | pass | four attacks 0/8 each; reference decoder 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | doubling `n` from 32 to 64 grows observations 193 to 385 and still verifies |
+| G8 | pass | 60/60 reorder/reflection invariance checks, 60/60 carried witnesses, 20/20 distinct unrelated keys |
+| G9 | **blocked/fail** | compact route verifies; 150 chars, 8 atoms, 38 estimated tokens, 245 ops; no scored oracle attempts |
+
+The four failed G6 attacks were smallest-value-magnitude outlier selection,
+smallest-step constant-noise greedy recovery, 256 uniform certificate restarts,
+and a sparse top-monomial ansatz. An earlier generator version made the planted
+values artificially small and the magnitude attack solved 8/8; sampling planted
+residues across the full field removed that leak before this report was retained.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+No oracle outcome exists yet. The bare harness wrote these script-owned error
+records and then correctly stopped as unreachable:
+
+| preset | seed | model | solved | why |
+|---|---:|---|---|---|
+| easy | 775365020 | OpenAI GPT-5.6 Terra | error | OpenRouter HTTP 403 total key limit |
+| easy | 1794220179 | OpenAI GPT-5.6 Terra | error | OpenRouter HTTP 403 total key limit |
+| easy | 1580483517 | Anthropic Claude Sonnet 5 | error | OpenRouter HTTP 403 total key limit |
+| easy | 540271021 | OpenAI GPT-5.6 Terra | error | OpenRouter HTTP 403 total key limit |
+
+| G9 arm | solved / scored attempts | API errors | conclusion |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | 0 / 0 | 4 | unavailable |
+| structural hint | 0 / 0 | 4 | unavailable; G9(b) not passed |
+| placebo | 0 / 0 | 4 | unavailable |
+
+`hinted - placebo` is undefined in evidentiary terms (stored as `0.0` only
+because neither arm has a scored denominator). No claim about hint usefulness is
+made.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import importlib.util
+
+path = "results/1401.1331/gen_1401_1331.py"
+spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("g", path)
+g = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
+spec.loader.exec_module(g)
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=7, **g.DIFFICULTY["easy"])
+text = g.render(inst)
+answer = g.parse_answer('<answer>' + __import__('json').dumps(inst['answer']) + '</answer>')
+assert g.verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+After a funded four-vendor hardening run has produced a `hardened` verdict and
+G9 has been patched from its transcripts, emit with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1401.1331 20 easy
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+The 0/200,000 guess rate concerns the explicitly bounded uniform certificate
+language after all public shape constraints; it is not a bound against a solver
+that notices the marker correlation. The marker-plus-differences route is
+intentionally fast and makes the family easy for any tool that automates it.
+The sample distribution is structured and does not inherit Theorem 9's
+random-point probability statement. I did not run Micciancio–Voulgaris CVP, LLL,
+or an optimized lattice package because the module is dependency-free; the exact
+signed-error decoder is the measured reference implementation. Finally, the
+oracle quota blocker means practical multi-model hardness—including the required
+hinted polarity test—is unknown. The family must not be submitted until those
+runs succeed and `selftest_report.json` has every gate passing.
