 ## What the family asks
 
-The source is Garcia-Morchon, Rietman, Shparlinski, and Tolhuizen,
-[“Interpolation and Approximation of Polynomials in Finite Fields over a Short
-Interval from Noisy Values”](https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.1331). An instance gives a
-prime `p`, a short interval, noisy residues `s` satisfying circular distance
-`|s-f(t)|_p <= Delta`, and several admissible progression steps. The solver must
-return the polynomial over `F_p` in one scaled falling-factorial basis
-`sum b_j binom(X/r,j)`. Verification evaluates this polynomial at every displayed
-point with exact modular arithmetic and checks every noise inequality. It never
-reads the planted answer.
-
-Generation is inverse. A step and a full-degree Newton coefficient vector are
-sampled first (equivalently, values on one progression are sampled and the exact
-forward-difference identity is inverted), then all noisy observations are formed.
-Every candidate progression has the same shape and every individual error uses
-the same `0..Delta` distribution. Only the useful progression has a common-error
-correlation; its residues consequently share the displayed marker class.
-
-## Why Track B, not Track A
-
-Section 1.2 defines circular residue distance and explains why coefficients below
-degree `k` must be known; when `h^k <= Delta`, recovery is not identifiable. This
-family uses `k=1`, publishes `f(0)=0`, and stays away from that regime. Lemma 7 and
-Theorem 9 are the decisive Step-0 facts: for fixed dimension the paper uses exact
-closest-vector decoding and obtains deterministic polynomial-time recovery from
-random short-interval samples. A Track A claim would therefore be false.
-
-The retained reference implementation performs exact signed-error enumeration
-and Newton interpolation, a fixed-dimensional bounded-distance decoder with cost
-`O((2*Delta+1)^d (d^2 + N d))`. On eight shipping instances it tried 4,086,290
-candidates, used 237,052,436 counted exact operations (29,631,555 mean), solved
-8/8, and took 43.41 seconds total (5.43 seconds mean). The compact route uses Section
-5.2's identity `d(x)=sum D^i d(0) binom(x,i)`: scan the 32 equal-shaped
-progressions modulo the marker, then build one six-level difference table. That
-is 245 counted operations. Millions of exact operations are easy for a program
-but unavailable in the evaluated no-tool context; 245 simple reductions and
-subtractions fit the stated cap if the structural clue is found.
-
-The paper also identifies regimes deliberately not claimed here: whole-field and
-long-interval variants admit earlier exponential-sum-based methods, Theorem 9
-assumes random points and fixed degree, and Section 6 gives practical lattice
-approximation heuristics. This generator is a structured native subfamily, not a
-sample from Theorem 9's random-point distribution.
-
-## Worked demo (`seed=2`)
-
-This is the complete rendered demo instance:
+The solver receives noisy values of the sparse polynomial
+`f(X)=a4*X^4+a3*X^3` over `GF(p)`, evaluated at ordinary integers in a short
+interval.  It must return `[a4,a3]`.  The checker evaluates that polynomial
+modulo `p` at every displayed point and compares the centered residue error to
+the exact integer bound `Delta`; it accepts any coefficient pair satisfying all
+samples and never reads the planted answer.  This is the native noisy
+interpolation problem defined in §1.2 of [Garcia-Morchon, Rietman, Shparlinski,
+and Tolhuizen](https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.1331), specialized to the known sparse
+support explicitly discussed there.
 
-```text
-Noisy polynomial interpolation over a short interval
+## Why Track B, and what is easy
 
-Let p = 1000003, a prime.  The finite field F_p is represented by the
-integers 0 through p-1.  For residues a,b define their circular distance as
-min((a-b) mod p, (b-a) mod p), an integer from 0 through (p-1)/2.
+Theorem 9 (§4.1), using the lattice of §3.2 and fixed-dimensional exact CVP in
+Lemma 7, gives a deterministic polynomial-time recovery algorithm in its
+parameter regime.  A Track A claim would therefore be false.  The paper also
+explains two important easy/impossible boundaries: low coefficients must be
+known with `h^k > Delta p^epsilon`, while exceptional polynomials and the HIMMO
+regime can make complete recovery impossible (§§1.2, 5, and 7).
 
